 ## Overview
-Application-Specific Extensions modify the standard 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) framework to handle non-RGB, non-photometric data or to extract explicit geometry from the implicit volumetric representation [4971-radiative-and-tomographic-gaussians.md], [5621-domain-specific-adaptations.md], [8498-surface-and-mesh-extraction.md]. While standard 3DGS optimizes for novel-view synthesis of RGB scenes using calibrated camera poses and photometric losses, these adaptations replace the projection target with volumetric supervision, voxel-space rasterization, or 3D geometric intersections [5621-domain-specific-adaptations.md], [8498-surface-and-mesh-extraction.md]. They solve the problem of reconstructing and visualizing high-dimensional or physically distinct data fields where traditional camera-based rendering is inapplicable or insufficient [G].
+Application-Specific Extensions modify the standard 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) framework to handle non-RGB, non-photometric data or to extract explicit geometry from the implicit volumetric representation [G]. While standard 3DGS optimizes for novel-view synthesis of RGB scenes using calibrated camera poses and photometric losses, these adaptations replace the projection target with volumetric supervision, voxel-space rasterization, or 3D geometric intersections [G]. They solve the problem of reconstructing and visualizing high-dimensional or physically distinct data fields where traditional camera-based rendering is inapplicable or insufficient [G].
 
 ## Shared principles
-All extensions in this family retain the core 3DGS primitive representation: a set of $M$ anisotropic 3D Gaussian kernels $\mathcal{G} = \{G_i\}_{i=1}^M$ [4971-radiative-and-tomographic-gaussians.md], [5621-domain-specific-adaptations.md]. Each kernel is defined by:
-*   $\mathbf{p}_i \in \mathbb{R}^3$: Position (mean) [4971-radiative-and-tomographic-gaussians.md].
-*   $\mathbf{R}_i \in \mathbb{R}^{3 \times 3}$: Rotation matrix [4971-radiative-and-tomographic-gaussians.md].
-*   $\mathbf{S}_i \in \mathbb{R}^{3 \times 3}$: Scale matrix [4971-radiative-and-tomographic-gaussians.md].
-*   The covariance matrix is $\mathbf{\Sigma}_i = \mathbf{R}_i \mathbf{S}_i \mathbf{S}_i^\top \mathbf{R}_i^\top$ [4971-radiative-and-tomographic-gaussians.md].
+All extensions in this family retain the core 3DGS primitive representation: a set of $M$ anisotropic 3D Gaussian kernels $\mathcal{G} = \{G_i\}_{i=1}^M$ [G]. Each kernel is defined by:
+*   $\mathbf{p}_i \in \mathbb{R}^3$: Position (mean) [G].
+*   $\mathbf{R}_i \in \mathbb{R}^{3 \times 3}$: Rotation matrix [G].
+*   $\mathbf{S}_i \in \mathbb{R}^{3 \times 3}$: Scale matrix [G].
+*   The covariance matrix is $\mathbf{\Sigma}_i = \mathbf{R}_i \mathbf{S}_i \mathbf{S}_i^\top \mathbf{R}_i^\top$ [G].
 
 The rendering pipeline's geometric projection remains consistent for ray-based methods: 3D Gaussians are transformed into ray space, and the projected 2D covariance $\mathbf{\hat{\Sigma}}_i$ is computed using the Jacobian $J$ of the affine approximation of the projection and the rotation part $W$ of the extrinsic matrix:
 $$ \mathbf{\hat{\Sigma}}_i = J W \mathbf{\Sigma}_i W^T J^T $$
