 ## Background
-3D Gaussian Splatting was originally introduced for real-time novel-view synthesis of RGB scenes [1795-3d-gaussian-splatting.md]. The extension to application-specific domains emerged as researchers sought to leverage the differentiable nature and fast rendering of Gaussians for scientific imaging and geometric analysis [G]. Radiative variants were developed to align the splatting model with X-ray physics [4971-radiative-and-tomographic-gaussians.md]. Domain-specific adaptations were created to handle medical and microscopy data where volumetric supervision is more appropriate than photometric error [5621-domain-specific-adaptations.md]. Surface extraction methods address the inherent lack of explicit topology in the Gaussian representation [8498-surface-and-mesh-extraction.md].
